     "- [ ] `stdlib/remaining-tasks-and-evidence/complete-schemaqueryworkflowaimetacryptoui-adapters` Complete schema/query/workflow/AI/meta/crypto/UI adapters\n\nStatus: Open\n\nEvidence baseline: [Module inventory](../../docs/phase-16-standard-library/README.md)\n\nClosing evidence: APIs consuming corresponding domain/runtime outcomes, with boundary failures exercised; STD10-STD13, STD15, STD18 and STD19 fixtures validate schemas before trusted decoding, canonical bytes, migration compatibility, parameterized queries, transaction commit/rollback/retry/isolation, durable workflow replay and in-flight migration, AI schema/taint/tool/review gates, generated-code rechecking, syntax context and pass invalidations, crypto lifecycle/hybrid binding/redaction, and typed UI events/render trust/accessibility/routes. Require actual provider execution and artifact consumers, not catalogue names or manifest presence. Reject each source-specific boundary failure in plan.md; unresolved provider, binding and equivalence decisions remain unassessed. STD15 requires actual syntax construction/inspection/transform APIs to preserve spans, hygiene, namespace, metadata and phase; C3 defines the underlying syntax representation and internal transform rules. Retain the library-call outcome separately from internal compiler transformations and public API publication. Explicit metadata updates may change the requested metadata while recording the change; they do not erase origin/hygiene/context or make stale facts current. Reject the following false substitutes: An internal compiler transform fixture does not prove the published syntax API actually calls it. Publishing an export or returning raw form data does not preserve syntax context. with-syntax-meta is an explicit update, not a promise that all metadata bytes are unchanged. The broad query outcome lowers/executes a typed query and validates rows/failures under declared transaction/provider semantics. STD11 additionally requires the standard transaction API to execute commit, rollback, retry and isolation/idempotency behavior. Retain that concrete lifecycle outcome under the existing adapters task; a provider transaction implementation may be shared but its invocation and observable transitions through the standard API require their own acceptance. Reject the following false substitutes: A parameterized SELECT returning typed rows proves no commit/rollback path. Transaction metadata or a named commit export does not execute the transaction. Retry must follow declared isolation, idempotency and provider failure policy; it is not an invented retry allowance. Retain the standard rendering boundary that distinguishes escaped text, trusted markup, binary media and native views, and enforces the selected sink's trust policy. Actual web DOM/render-event execution is one qualified consumer; native/terminal/media adapters have their declared target rules. Reuse actual adapter checks without treating a web-only unsafe-DOM fixture or public rendering API inventory as universal rendering conformance. Reject the following false substitutes: A typed render export alone does not reject unsafe markup at an actual sink. HTML escaping is not a universal native-view, media or terminal trust rule. An event/schema fixture does not establish the rendering kind distinction or adapter invocation."},
